---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const breadcrumb = {
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://haloglory.com/en' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Work', item: 'https://haloglory.com/en/work' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Jakarta Feminist', item: 'https://haloglory.com/en/work/jakarta-feminist' },
  ],
};
const waCta = "https://wa.me/6285927277560?text=Hi!%20I%20read%20the%20Jakarta%20Feminist%20case%20study%20and%20I'd%20like%20to%20discuss%20a%20project...";
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="Case Study: Jakarta Feminist — 3 Websites, 5 Years | MGL"
  description="A three-website contract that became five years of support. The biggest lesson came from a client who refused features."
  idPath="/portofolio/jakarta-feminist"
  enPath="/en/work/jakarta-feminist"
  extraSchema={[breadcrumb]}
>
  <main id="page-case-en" class="page">
    <section class="hero wrap">
      <div class="shead reveal">
        <div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">// Case Study</span><span class="folio">01</span></div>
      </div>
      <h1 class="h1 reveal d1" style="max-width:20ch;margin-top:18px;font-size:clamp(32px,4.4vw,58px)">Three Websites, Five Years: What Happens When a Client Refuses Features</h1>
      <p class="lead reveal d2" style="margin-top:22px">It started as a contract to build three websites. It turned into almost five years of support — and changed how I judge my own work.</p>
      <div class="reveal d2" style="margin-top:38px;border:1px solid var(--hair)">
        <img src="/img/work/jakarta-feminist.webp" alt="Jakarta Feminist project screenshot" width="1200" height="579" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block">
      </div>
      <div class="deflist reveal d3" style="margin-top:34px">
        <div><b>Client</b><p>Jakarta Feminist — a feminist organisation in Jakarta</p></div><div><b>Industry</b><p>Non-profit / advocacy</p></div><div><b>Scope</b><p>Three websites, Wix Editor to WordPress migration</p></div><div><b>Duration</b><p>Almost five years of support</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <hr class="hair" style="margin:0 var(--gut)">
    <section class="pad-sm wrap">
      <div class="shead reveal"><div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">// 02 — Context</span></div></div>
      <div class="persp">
        <div class="reveal"><p class="big">A three-website contract that never really ended.</p></div>
        <div class="reveal d1">
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">I was contracted to build three websites. Technically, that work had an end date. What happened was the opposite: the relationship ran for almost five years, through Women's March Jakarta, FemFest, and a number of other campaigns, each with its own needs.</p>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">The work included pulling editorial content, events, and resources into a single mobilisation hub — and moving them off Wix Editor onto WordPress so they could manage their own content without depending on anyone.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="pad-sm wrap">
      <div class="shead reveal"><div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">// 03 — What I didn't expect</span></div></div>
      <div class="persp">
        <div class="reveal"><p class="big">They refused almost everything I offered.</p></div>
        <div class="reveal d1">
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">This client knew exactly what they wanted. What I didn't expect was how firmly they refused what they didn't want. Every time I proposed a feature that sounded clever, the question was the same: who will use this, and what happens if no one does?</p>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">Most of my proposals didn't pass that question. They wanted it simple, they wanted it to work, and they wanted decisions made from the input of the people actually using the site — not from me, and not from trends.</p>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">For a while I saw that as a limitation. A few years later I realised it was the discipline I've most rarely found in other clients.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="pad-sm wrap">
      <div class="shead reveal"><div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">// 04 — Approach</span></div></div>
      <div class="persp">
        <div class="reveal"><p class="big">A feature you don't build is a feature you don't have to maintain.</p></div>
        <div class="reveal d1">
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">The consequence only showed in year three. A site built with few moving parts is a site a new person can still pick up when staff change. No feature nobody understands. No section everyone is afraid to touch because they don't know what will break.</p>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">The trade-off is real: a few things take more manual steps than they should, and I still think some of my old proposals were right. But surviving five years is stronger evidence than my argument.</p>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:20px">This became the foundation of how I work now: ask first who will maintain something, before agreeing to build it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <hr class="hair" style="margin:0 var(--gut)">
    <section class="pad-sm wrap">
      <div class="shead reveal">
        <div class="label">
          <div>
            <span class="eyebrow">// 05 — Results</span>
            <h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:18px">What I can stand behind.</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="folio">05</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stats reveal">
        <div class="stat"><div class="num" style="font-size:clamp(26px,2.6vw,38px)">Almost 5 years</div><p style="margin-top:10px">Ongoing support, far beyond the original contract scope.</p></div><div class="stat"><div class="num" style="font-size:clamp(26px,2.6vw,38px)">3 websites</div><p style="margin-top:10px">Built and maintained, including a Wix Editor to WordPress migration.</p></div><div class="stat"><div class="num" style="font-size:clamp(26px,2.6vw,38px)">Supported major campaigns</div><p style="margin-top:10px">Women's March Jakarta, FemFest, and more.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:28px;font-size:15px;color:var(--ink-soft);max-width:70ch">There are no traffic or conversion numbers here because I don't hold the data. The measure of success for this project is the hardest to fake: the sites are still in use, and the team can still manage them themselves.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="pad-sm wrap">
      <div class="reveal" style="display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <a href="/en/services/wordpress-development" class="btn ghost">WordPress website service <span class="arr">↗</span></a>
        <a href="https://jakartafeminist.com/" class="btn ghost" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit the site <span class="arr">↗</span></a>
        <a href="/en/work" class="btn ghost">All projects <span class="arr">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="cta-ink pad">
      <img class="bgimg" src="/img/cta-bg.webp" width="1440" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="wrap" style="position:relative">
        <span class="eyebrow reveal">// Let's talk</span>
        <h2 class="h2 reveal d1" style="margin:24px 0 36px;max-width:20ch">Got a problem worth solving?</h2>
        <div class="reveal d2">
          <a href={waCta} class="btn on-ink" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-wa="cta" data-page="/en/work/jakarta-feminist">
            Free Consultation<span class="arr">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
